page cmd_destroy {
  title = "vmlab destroy"
  chapter_header "vmlab destroy" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "3 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab destroy` stops the lab in the current directory and deletes
    everything it materialised: the clones, the container overlays and named
    volumes, the lab-local state under `.vmlab/`, and the dynamic network
    configuration. The lab file is untouched. The next
    [vmlab up](cmd_up) starts from fresh clones and runs first-boot
    provisioning again.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab destroy" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  h2 "What it does"

  p "The verb works in this order:"

  list {
    style = :numbered
    li "It withdraws every SSH alias the lab publishes, while the stanzas in the managed `~/.ssh/config` block still resolve, so any `ssh` multiplexer holding a connection is told to exit. See [vmlab ssh-config](cmd_ssh_config)."
    li "With a lab daemon running, it asks the daemon to destroy the lab. The daemon stops every workspace syncer, force-stops every machine, waits up to 30 seconds for each to settle, then removes `.vmlab/` and each machine's runtime directory."
    li "With no daemon running, it removes `.vmlab/` itself if the directory exists."
    li "It asks the supervisor to release the lab, which reaps the lab daemon."
  }

  p "The last line printed is `lab \"<name>\" destroyed`."

  p <<DOC
    Everything a machine had that was not in its template goes: [snapshots](guide_snapshots)
    live inside the clones, so they go with them, and a machine marked
    diverged by [vmlab machine repair-agent](cmd_machine) comes back on its
    template's sealed agent. The `vmlab dev use` selection recorded in
    `.vmlab/` is forgotten too, and so is the workspace sync ledger.
  DOC

  callout "The workspace survives, the guest tree does not" {
    kind = :warning
    body = "A dev machine's source lives in the host workspace directory, which `destroy` never touches. The guest copy is deleted with the clone. Any guest edit the syncer has not carried to the host is lost, so run `vmlab dev sync flush` first if the syncer was halted."
  }

  p <<DOC
    Templates in the store are never written to by a lab and are not removed
    here; use [vmlab template](cmd_template) for that. To destroy a lab by
    name from any directory use [vmlab lab destroy](cmd_lab).
  DOC

  h2 "Examples"

  p "Tear a lab down and rebuild it from scratch:"
  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab destroy
vmlab up
SRC
  }

  h2 "Exit status"

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0 when the lab's state was removed, whether or not a
    daemon was running. A lab directory that cannot be found, a machine that
    cannot be stopped, or a `.vmlab/` directory that cannot be removed exits
    1 (`failed`). A usage error exits 2.
  DOC
}
